republic-overreach.md:1: argue/readings: S1 is read 2 ways: republic-overreach.logic.r3 has "∀x ((just(x) → unlike_only(x)) ∧ (unjust(x) → both(x)))"; republic-overreach.logic.r3.2 and republic-overreach.logic.r3.3 have nothing from it; they differ when nothing is true, which the second allows
republic-overreach.md:1: argue/answered: S1: answered no, which keeps republic-overreach.logic.r3, to: Suppose nothing is true. Is S1, "We may put the matter thus, I said—the just does not desire more than his like but more than his unlike, whereas the unjust desires more than both his like and his unlike?", true then?
republic-overreach.logic.r3: argue/readings: what the text leaves unstated is read 3 ways: republic-overreach.logic.r3 has "∀x (wise(x) → knowing(x))" and "∀x ((unlike_only(x) → like_wise(x)) ∧ (both(x) → like_ignorant(x)))"; republic-overreach.logic.r3.2 has "∀y (wise(y) ∧ good(y) → unlike_only(y)) → ∀x (unlike_only(x) → like_wise(x))" and "∀y (bad(y) ∧ ignorant(y) → both(y)) → ∀x (both(x) → like_ignorant(x))"; they differ when nothing is true, which the second allows; republic-overreach.logic.r3.3 has "∀x (unlike_only(x) → like_wise(x))" and "∀x (both(x) → like_ignorant(x))"
republic-overreach.logic.r3: argue/readings: U1: republic-overreach.logic.r3 takes for granted "for every x, if x is wise, then x is a man who has knowledge"; republic-overreach.logic.r3.2 and republic-overreach.logic.r3.3 do not
republic-overreach.logic.r3: argue/answered: U1: answered yes, which keeps republic-overreach.logic.r3, to: Does the text's argument take this for granted, whether or not it says so: "for every x, if x is wise, then x is a man who has knowledge"? Answer yes if the author would accept it as part of the argument.
